WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.320
My name's Laurence Scott
and in my humble opinion,

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.760
I think that social media
is changing the ways

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.520
both in which we experience
the emotions of other people

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.360
and how we express
our emotions publicly.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.240
I think the main thing you have
to think about in social media

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.120
is that our emotions
are a valuable resource.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.520
So it behoves the people who are
designing social media networks -

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.480
such as Twitter, Facebook -

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.560
to make our emotions
as clearly obvious

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.440
and evident to others as possible.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:35.960
So you may have noticed on Facebook,

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.640
one of the first questions
you're greeted with is,

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:39.880
"What's on your mind?"

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.960
Couple of years ago I noticed
sort of a comedy of errors

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.160
that's brewing in our emotional
landscape with social media.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:51.320
So in the same year, Facebook finally
rolled out its six reaction features.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:55.600
So instead of just simply liking
things that your friends have posted,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.480
you could add the heart
or the sad face or the angry face.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.160
Mark Zuckerberg at the time said,

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.840
"What people want
to do is express empathy."

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:10.520
But this empathy was again channelled
into a discrete set of emotions

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.320
that weren't limitless or boundless.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.520
Now why this is a funny sort
of irony,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.760
is that in that same year
a robot was released onto the market

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.520
by a company called Hanson Robotics,
and the robot was called Pepper.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.680
And robots such as these were being
trained to recognise and I quote,

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.680
"62 different facial and neck
architectural features of humans."

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.000
So while the robots were learning
62 different ways to recognise us,

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.360
and to be able to tell
what emotions we were feeling,

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.600
we were being allowed a range
of six emotions on social media.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.080
I'm sure you've noticed this too,

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.160
that on social media
we're asked to experience

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.120
two completely different kinds
of emotions one after the other.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.280
So on your timeline you may see
that your best friend's child

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:00.000
is sort of toddling around
on the carpet.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.200
You may feel the warmth
and excitement of that.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:05.640
And then the next post
could be something about

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.720
7,000 killed in an earthquake.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:11.320
The way that the timeline
and the news feeds are structured

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.280
really have no emotional
consistency at all.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.200
So we're often being jerked
in our effective responses

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:20.840
from one emotion
to the next quite quickly.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.640
And I think this century
will be about how do we cope

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.280
with these quick gear changes
in our emotional lives.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:33.440
Where people's internal landscapes
are being altered

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.200
very, very radically
from one emotion to the next.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.480
Really the name of the game
in social media commerce

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:45.720
is to make your time on the site
as maximum as possible.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.600
So a lot of the ways
that the websites are designed

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:51.200
to encourage you to stay on it,
to return to it,

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:53.800
and how they do that
is to create quite addictive

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.840
little algorithms, such as
there's one where on Twitter

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.520
there's a bit of a delay before how
many notifications you have come up.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.600
And there's a very human,
basic response there,

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.360
where we wait to see something
that is variable and changing.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.640
We get a little hit of dopamine,

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:15.360
when there's a category of data
that is changing that we can monitor.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.800
So there's that brief moment
of excitement that we all know

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.440
even when we're just
checking an email,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.600
"How many new emails do I have?"
"How many notifications?"

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:27.760
And that little pause encourages in
us that sort of sense of anticipation

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.560
that is then rewarded
when we see the actual number.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.440
So in this way, social media
is trying to tap in

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.800
to very fundamental things about
how our psychologies work

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:39.720
and our emotional lives
are structured.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.280
Thanks for watching!

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.440
If you enjoyed that, be sure
to check out these videos next.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:46.920
And if you haven't already,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.840
hit the subscribe button
